1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,781
Ravi's pull request had three commits
and two files, and it was approved.

2
00:00:08,821 --> 00:00:13,339
A commit is one saved snapshot of the
project, and a pull request is GitHub's

3
00:00:13,379 --> 00:00:17,364
proposal to merge a branch of commits
into another branch, its base.

4
00:00:17,404 --> 00:00:22,040
He pushed one small commit. Now the page
shows more than five hundred files and

5
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:25,118
commits by a colleague who
never touched his branch.

6
00:00:25,158 --> 00:00:29,853
He suspects that somebody rewrote main,
or that somebody changed the base of his

7
00:00:29,893 --> 00:00:30,623
pull request.

8
00:00:30,963 --> 00:00:36,076
And one detail that he mentions last, as
if it were good news: the approval still

9
00:00:36,116 --> 00:00:39,003
stands. Is that good
news? Hold that question.

10
00:00:39,343 --> 00:00:45,378
Quick quiz, before any cause is named. Which
record would test Ravi's first suspect,

11
00:00:45,418 --> 00:00:51,092
a rewritten main? A, the reflog of
origin/main, Git's local record of where the

12
00:00:51,132 --> 00:00:55,720
server's main has been. B, the
pull request page. C, git status.

13
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:56,588
Your answer?

14
00:01:01,428 --> 00:01:05,130
A. A rewrite would stand in
that reflog as a forced update.

15
00:01:05,170 --> 00:01:07,413
Whether one does, we find out later.

16
00:01:09,253 --> 00:01:13,523
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

17
00:01:13,563 --> 00:01:17,834
This is a debrief of Incident
6, incidents/06-pr-500-changes.

18
00:01:17,874 --> 00:01:23,628
You must have generated and attempted it,
with your hypotheses written down before

19
00:01:23,668 --> 00:01:28,080
you tested them. Have you? If you
haven't, stop the video here.

20
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:32,453
The cause is named in the next section.

21
00:01:32,793 --> 00:01:37,709
From video 125 you know what a pull request
page computes: a commit list, which is

22
00:01:37,749 --> 00:01:42,664
everything reachable from the head and not
from the base, and a diff, which is the

23
00:01:42,704 --> 00:01:45,867
three-dot comparison from
the merge base to the head.

24
00:01:45,907 --> 00:01:50,580
The head is the branch that offers the
commits, and the merge base is the most

25
00:01:50,620 --> 00:01:52,756
recent commit both branches contain.

26
00:01:53,096 --> 00:01:57,727
So when the page changes dramatically,
one of three things moved: the head, the

27
00:01:57,767 --> 00:02:02,458
base, or the merge base between them. The
incident is an exercise in finding out

28
00:02:02,498 --> 00:02:07,306
which, with Git commands on the developer's
machine, and then repairing the branch

29
00:02:07,346 --> 00:02:09,258
without closing the pull request.

30
00:02:09,598 --> 00:02:14,838
In the lab the pull request is simulated
as main..head and main...head, which is

31
00:02:14,878 --> 00:02:16,224
what GitHub computes.

32
00:02:18,064 --> 00:02:19,477
After this video you can:

33
00:02:19,817 --> 00:02:23,860
List the mechanisms that make a small
pull request show hundreds of files.

34
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:28,121
Name the Git command that tests each
mechanism on the developer's machine.

35
00:02:28,461 --> 00:02:31,549
Attribute the extra changes to
the commit that brought them in.

36
00:02:31,889 --> 00:02:35,922
Rebuild the branch so that the pull
request shows only its own change.

37
00:02:36,262 --> 00:02:38,796
Explain the consequence
of leaving it as it is.

38
00:02:40,636 --> 00:02:45,675
The candidates. The page shows a three-dot
diff: either an endpoint or the merge

39
00:02:45,715 --> 00:02:51,009
base isn't what the author thinks. The
catalog of video 184 lists the mechanisms for

40
00:02:51,049 --> 00:02:55,200
"a pull request shows hundreds of
changes". The wrong base branch.

41
00:02:55,240 --> 00:03:00,470
The head branch reused after a squash merge.
The branch rebased or force-pushed, or

42
00:03:00,510 --> 00:03:04,979
its base rewritten. Every line changed,
by line endings or a formatter.

43
00:03:05,019 --> 00:03:09,868
A lock file or generated file. And a
merge of the wrong branch into the head.

44
00:03:10,208 --> 00:03:15,990
Each has a local test. A rewritten main:
the reflog of origin/main and the merge

45
00:03:16,030 --> 00:03:20,283
base. A force-pushed head: the
reflog of the pushing clone.

46
00:03:20,323 --> 00:03:24,358
A merge: git log --merges over
the pull request's range.

47
00:03:24,398 --> 00:03:29,598
Reformatting: git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol
--stat against the plain diff.

48
00:03:29,638 --> 00:03:33,164
A wrong base: the range
from each candidate base.

49
00:03:33,504 --> 00:03:38,315
The root cause here. A topic branch, a
branch for one piece of work, was aimed at

50
00:03:38,355 --> 00:03:42,208
main and updated from develop, an
unreleased long-running branch.

51
00:03:42,248 --> 00:03:47,000
A merge makes every commit of the merged
branch an ancestor of the result, and a

52
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:51,552
pull request lists everything reachable
from the head and not from the base.

53
00:03:51,592 --> 00:03:54,546
Layer: Git. GitHub displayed
the branch correctly.

54
00:03:54,886 --> 00:03:59,793
Why "one small commit" felt true. git log
--first-parent shows the branch as Ravi

55
00:03:59,833 --> 00:04:04,802
experienced it: his commits and one merge.
A merge commit has two parents, and the

56
00:04:04,842 --> 00:04:08,894
five hundred files came in through
the second parent of that merge.

57
00:04:09,234 --> 00:04:13,889
The tempting fix, and why it is wrong. git
revert -m 1 of the merge commit, which

58
00:04:13,929 --> 00:04:18,178
adds a commit that undoes the merge:
no forced push, and the diff shrinks.

59
00:04:18,218 --> 00:04:22,641
It's wrong here for two reasons. The
commits of develop stay ancestors of the

60
00:04:22,681 --> 00:04:24,844
branch, so the commit list stays long.

61
00:04:25,184 --> 00:04:29,988
And once this branch is merged, main contains
those commits together with a commit

62
00:04:30,028 --> 00:04:34,596
that undoes them, so the later release
of develop brings nothing: the re-merge

63
00:04:34,636 --> 00:04:39,322
problem. The textbook's sentence for it:
a reverted merge isn't a removed merge.

64
00:04:39,662 --> 00:04:43,919
The repair. The branch has one author,
so it's rebuilt: anchor the current tip,

65
00:04:43,959 --> 00:04:48,378
replay what came after the merge onto the
merge's first parent, and publish with a

66
00:04:48,418 --> 00:04:52,131
lease, a forced push that's refused
if the server's branch has moved.

67
00:04:52,471 --> 00:04:57,499
GitHub, not Git: the approval. Whether an
approval survives a push depends on the

68
00:04:57,539 --> 00:05:02,441
rules: "dismiss stale pull request
approvals" removes it when the diff changes.

69
00:05:02,481 --> 00:05:07,696
Here an approval given to two files stood
on 504. Treat it as void and review again.

70
00:05:07,736 --> 00:05:10,762
So that detail from the
opening wasn't good news.

71
00:05:11,102 --> 00:05:17,139
Severity. SEV 3. Nothing was merged. The
near miss is the standing approval, not the

72
00:05:17,179 --> 00:05:17,935
file count.

73
00:05:19,775 --> 00:05:24,187
Picture the pull request as the answer to
one question: "what would arrive in the

74
00:05:24,227 --> 00:05:28,584
base if this were merged now?" Everything
the head can reach and the base can't.

75
00:05:28,624 --> 00:05:32,981
A merge commit is a door: whatever is
behind its second parent walks in with it.

76
00:05:33,321 --> 00:05:36,954
Ravi opened a door to develop to
get one thing he wanted from it.

77
00:05:36,994 --> 00:05:41,304
Everything else on develop came through
the same door, and the page showed it

78
00:05:41,344 --> 00:05:41,926
faithfully.

79
00:05:42,266 --> 00:05:46,890
Where the picture breaks: you can close
a door, and you can't un-open a merge by

80
00:05:46,930 --> 00:05:51,787
adding a commit. A revert changes the content
back and leaves the ancestry in place.

81
00:05:51,827 --> 00:05:56,627
Only a branch that never contained the merge
is a branch without the door, which is

82
00:05:56,667 --> 00:05:58,259
why the repair is a rebuild.

83
00:05:58,599 --> 00:06:02,711
For the method, one habit: read the
commit list, not only the files.

84
00:06:02,751 --> 00:06:07,656
The files said "something large changed".
The commit list, with its authors, said

85
00:06:07,696 --> 00:06:07,961
what.

86
00:06:08,301 --> 00:06:14,144
Try it now. Thirty seconds, read-only.
In any repository you have, run git log

87
00:06:14,184 --> 00:06:20,102
--merges --oneline -5. Each line is a
door. Say out loud which branch walked in

88
00:06:20,142 --> 00:06:21,008
through one.

89
00:06:25,848 --> 00:06:30,734
A merge's subject usually names the branch
that came in, as "Merge branch 'develop'"

90
00:06:30,774 --> 00:06:33,315
does here. That reading
found today's cause.

91
00:06:35,155 --> 00:06:40,072
Before the demo, what we know so far is what
the page shows: yesterday three commits

92
00:06:40,112 --> 00:06:44,910
and two files, today more than five hundred
files. The graph of the branch, before

93
00:06:44,950 --> 00:06:47,507
and after the rebuild,
is drawn in the demo.

94
00:06:49,347 --> 00:06:54,278
From here on the screen shows the solution.
Is your own command log beside you?

95
00:06:56,618 --> 00:06:57,930
Then compare as we go.

96
00:06:58,270 --> 00:06:59,659
What the pull request shows.

97
00:06:59,999 --> 00:07:05,693
Eight commits, three of them by Asha. 504
files. And one line that already narrows

98
00:07:05,733 --> 00:07:10,029
it: 99.2 percent of the changed
files are under tests/golden/.

99
00:07:10,069 --> 00:07:15,344
Read the commit list with the authors:
one subject begins with "Merge branch

100
00:07:15,384 --> 00:07:16,113
'develop'".

101
00:07:16,453 --> 00:07:18,874
Hypotheses, tested one at a time.

102
00:07:19,214 --> 00:07:23,718
Was main rewritten? The reflog of
origin/main and the merge base say no.

103
00:07:23,758 --> 00:07:28,956
Was the head branch force-pushed? The pushing
clone's reflog shows ordinary pushes.

104
00:07:28,996 --> 00:07:33,058
Two of Ravi's own suspects are
ruled out with read-only commands.

105
00:07:33,398 --> 00:07:37,433
Hypothesis 3: another branch was
merged into the head branch.

106
00:07:37,473 --> 00:07:42,108
Predict what git log --merges over
the pull request's range will list.

107
00:07:42,448 --> 00:07:48,250
One merge commit, 461c3ea, by Ravi, with
the tip of develop, 069daa8, as its second

108
00:07:48,290 --> 00:07:52,895
parent. The graph shows the three
commits that came in through it.

109
00:07:53,235 --> 00:07:55,624
Attribution: which commit
brought the files?

110
00:07:55,964 --> 00:08:00,782
The file count belongs to the commits
behind the second parent, and Ravi's reflog

111
00:08:00,822 --> 00:08:05,099
names the command that made the merge:
a pull of develop without rebase.

112
00:08:05,139 --> 00:08:08,397
Now say the root cause in one
sentence, with its layer.

113
00:08:10,737 --> 00:08:15,411
Root cause, with its layer: Git. A topic
branch aimed at main was updated from

114
00:08:15,451 --> 00:08:18,252
develop. GitHub displayed
the branch correctly.

115
00:08:18,592 --> 00:08:22,925
Here's the branch as a graph. Neither
the base nor the merge base moved.

116
00:08:22,965 --> 00:08:25,416
The head grew a second line of ancestors.

117
00:08:25,756 --> 00:08:31,004
Rebuild, in Ravi's clone. 🟢 for the anchor.
🟡 CAUTION: git rebase --onto replaces

118
00:08:31,044 --> 00:08:36,162
the commits after the merge with new ones.
The two arguments: the merge's first

119
00:08:36,202 --> 00:08:40,145
parent as the new base, and the
merge itself as the boundary.

120
00:08:46,485 --> 00:08:51,107
One commit replayed. Four
commits in a line, no merge.

121
00:08:51,147 --> 00:08:56,805
The last one is now 1981450: a new
ID, because its parent changed.

122
00:08:57,145 --> 00:08:58,998
Check the result before publishing.

123
00:08:59,338 --> 00:09:04,617
The replayed commit carries the same change
as the original: one pair with an equals

124
00:09:04,657 --> 00:09:09,873
sign. Two files. And develop is no longer
an ancestor of the branch: exit status 1,

125
00:09:09,913 --> 00:09:12,026
which here is the result you want.

126
00:09:12,366 --> 00:09:16,796
Publish. 🔴 DANGEROUS: git push
--force-with-lease --force-if-includes.

127
00:09:16,836 --> 00:09:21,968
What it changes: the server's topic branch,
to a commit that doesn't descend from

128
00:09:22,008 --> 00:09:27,268
the old one. What it can destroy: commits
on that server branch that aren't in this

129
00:09:27,308 --> 00:09:32,569
clone. Preview: git fetch, then look at
what the server's branch has that you lack.

130
00:09:32,609 --> 00:09:36,208
Recovery: the old tip is
anchored as rescue/with-develop.

131
00:09:36,248 --> 00:09:40,168
When appropriate: for a topic
branch with one author, as here.

132
00:09:40,508 --> 00:09:42,516
Verify, from the reviewer's clone.

133
00:09:42,856 --> 00:09:47,828
Four commits, all Ravi's. Two files. The
check passes, including two lines about

134
00:09:47,868 --> 00:09:51,086
what wasn't touched: develop
and main on the server.

135
00:09:54,426 --> 00:09:57,600
The consequence of leaving it,
or of reverting the merge.

136
00:09:57,640 --> 00:10:01,659
In the lab's failure scenario the
merge was reverted instead of removed.

137
00:10:01,699 --> 00:10:06,339
Later the pull request is merged into main,
and then develop is released into main.

138
00:10:06,379 --> 00:10:09,440
Predict what the release merge
prints. Say it out loud.

139
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:19,242
"Already up to date." The release of develop
brings nothing, because its commits are

140
00:10:19,282 --> 00:10:23,292
already ancestors of main, together
with the commit that undid them.

141
00:10:23,332 --> 00:10:28,294
The listing of tests shows no golden fixtures.
An entire release has silently become

142
00:10:28,334 --> 00:10:33,118
empty. And the check script says it in one
line: a reverted merge isn't a removed

143
00:10:33,158 --> 00:10:33,475
merge.

144
00:10:36,815 --> 00:10:41,921
The messages. To Ravi: GitHub showed what
the branch contained, here is the reflog

145
00:10:41,961 --> 00:10:47,066
line, and the last commit has a new ID. To
the lead: the near miss is the standing

146
00:10:47,106 --> 00:10:52,087
approval, not the file count. Had it been
merged, 500 fixtures and an unreleased

147
00:10:52,127 --> 00:10:56,229
tokenizer would have reached main
under an approval for two files.

148
00:10:58,069 --> 00:11:02,503
Blaming the platform or a rewritten
main first. Root cause: the page is a

149
00:11:02,543 --> 00:11:07,591
computation over the head, the base and the
merge base, and the reflogs showed that

150
00:11:07,631 --> 00:11:10,595
neither the base nor the
head had been rewritten.

151
00:11:10,935 --> 00:11:14,388
Updating a topic branch from
a branch other than its base.

152
00:11:14,428 --> 00:11:19,087
Root cause: a merge makes every commit
of the merged branch an ancestor of the

153
00:11:19,127 --> 00:11:21,797
result, so all of it
enters the pull request.

154
00:11:22,137 --> 00:11:26,976
Reverting the merge to shrink the diff.
Root cause: the revert changes content and

155
00:11:27,016 --> 00:11:31,914
leaves ancestry, so the commit list stays
long and a later merge of the same branch

156
00:11:31,954 --> 00:11:32,806
brings nothing.

157
00:11:33,146 --> 00:11:38,235
Reading only the changed files. Root cause:
the commit list, with authors, names the

158
00:11:38,275 --> 00:11:42,753
foreign commits directly; the file view
only says that something is large.

159
00:11:43,093 --> 00:11:47,550
Letting the old approval stand. Root cause:
the approval was given to a different

160
00:11:47,590 --> 00:11:51,770
diff, and whether it is dismissed depends
on a rule that may not be enabled.

161
00:11:53,610 --> 00:11:58,171
Now, out of the lab. A search team keeps
a long-running develop branch for a

162
00:11:58,211 --> 00:12:02,470
tokenizer migration that regenerates
five hundred golden test fixtures.

163
00:12:02,510 --> 00:12:07,375
An engineer working on snippet highlighting,
on a branch aimed at main, needs one

164
00:12:07,415 --> 00:12:12,219
helper that exists only on develop, and
pulls develop into his branch to get it.

165
00:12:12,559 --> 00:12:15,877
His reviewer, the next morning,
doesn't approve again.

166
00:12:15,917 --> 00:12:20,976
She reads the commit list and sees a
colleague's name on three commits and a merge

167
00:12:21,016 --> 00:12:26,138
subject that names develop. She runs git
log --merges over the pull request's range

168
00:12:26,178 --> 00:12:30,802
in her clone, sends him the one line,
and asks him not to revert the merge.

169
00:12:30,842 --> 00:12:36,025
They rebuild the branch together: an anchor,
one rebase --onto, three checks, a push

170
00:12:36,065 --> 00:12:40,876
with a lease. The helper he needed is
cherry-picked as its own reviewed commit

171
00:12:40,916 --> 00:12:41,373
instead.

172
00:12:41,713 --> 00:12:46,441
The team adds two lines to its contribution
guide. Update a topic branch from the

173
00:12:46,481 --> 00:12:50,384
branch it will be merged into, and
name that branch in the command.

174
00:12:50,424 --> 00:12:55,329
And the repository's ruleset gets the option
that dismisses stale approvals when the

175
00:12:55,369 --> 00:13:00,214
diff changes, because an approval for two
files had stood on five hundred and four.

176
00:13:02,054 --> 00:13:08,493
Your turn. Do Lab 37.3, "A pull request
suddenly shows 500 unrelated changes

177
00:13:08,533 --> 00:13:14,972
(incident 6)", in
lab-manual/m37-incident-drills-platform.md, from a freshly

178
00:13:15,012 --> 00:13:16,507
generated sandbox.

179
00:13:16,847 --> 00:13:21,494
Before any command, write four mechanisms
that could produce the symptom and the

180
00:13:21,534 --> 00:13:26,240
local command that tests each. Before the
rebuild, write the two arguments of git

181
00:13:26,280 --> 00:13:30,868
rebase --onto you'll use and what each
stands for, and predict the commit count

182
00:13:30,908 --> 00:13:35,673
afterwards. In the lab's failure scenario,
